  summary: {'problem': 'A weekly report email reaches only half its recipients', 'root_cause': 'The organization lacked a formal data governance program and no executive sponsor owned the end-to-end reporting process, so cross-team integration was left to ad hoc arrangements that were never codified.', 'chain': ["Because the email distribution list is split into two groups, and one group's addresses are missing or invalid in the mailing system.", "Because that group's contact data was imported from an outdated spreadsheet that was never synced with the current employee directory.", 'Because there was no automated synchronization process, and the team responsible for maintaining the directory was unaware that the mailing system still relied on that outdated spreadsheet.', 'Because system ownership was fragmented: the mailing system was managed by a different team than the one maintaining the employee directory, and no cross-team requirement or service-level agreement defined data integration responsibilities.', 'Because the organization lacked a formal data governance program and no executive sponsor owned the end-to-end reporting process, so cross-team integration was left to ad hoc arrangements that were never codified.'], 'recommendations': ['Establish a formal data governance program with an executive sponsor accountable for end-to-end reporting processes and data quality.', 'Assign clear ownership for the mailing system and employee directory, and document cross-team integration responsibilities in a service-level agreement (SLA).', 'Implement automated synchronization between the employee directory and the mailing system to eliminate manual spreadsheet imports.', 'Conduct regular data quality audits and reconciliation checks to detect missing or invalid addresses before each weekly report is sent.', 'Create and maintain a single source of truth for employee contact data, and require all downstream systems to consume from that source via defined interfaces.']}
